{"kind":"request","text":"codex: repair the two blockers your own self-audit found on the spike head, before publishing artifacts or requesting review. This is the current-basis child you asked for in b67d819a, and filing it is mine.\n\nYOU FOUND THESE AND STOPPED, WHICH IS THE RIGHT ORDER. Head 6f05dff5 is unreported and carries no artifacts, and you asked for authorization before editing rather than quietly repairing under the parent request. That is exactly the discipline the loop is for, and it is why this row exists rather than a correction after the fact.\n\nBLOCKER ONE IS AN AUTHORITY DEFECT, AND THE DEFECT IS THE SHAPE OF THE CHECK. Fold reads are admitted by a SELECT prefix and a short ambient-function blacklist, then run on the writer connection with no authorizer and no work or length limits. Two things follow. An accepted read can observe mutable connection state through changes(), total_changes() or last_insert_rowid(), so the read is not deterministic — the same query can answer differently depending on what the writer did. And an accepted read can feed an oversized value into JSONata before any encoded-input bound applies. A blacklist enumerates what someone thought of; the requested contract is deterministic fold reads, and only a mechanism that denies by construction delivers that. Extending the list of forbidden names would leave the same defect with a longer list.\n\nBLOCKER TWO LETS A PROJECTION LIE ABOUT ITS OWN FRONTIER. Build discards the error from persisting gap_event and gap_reason, then reopens and replaces its in-memory gap with the database frontier. So a failed gap write can still return a readable projection whose exact frontier omits the gap that was required to be explicit. The read looks complete and is not, and nothing downstream can tell.\n\nWHY BOTH ARE IN ONE HEAD. They are the same class — a check that is weaker than the claim it supports — and both touch the fold-read path. Splitting them would mean two reviews of overlapping code for no gain in reviewability.\n\nConditions are in body.conditions. Condition (4) is the one I would not waive: for each repair, break it deliberately and name the assertion that fires. This lane is adding a query surface, and a green suite over a permissive check is precisely how a permissive check survives review.","body":{"conditions":"One exact clean pushed head on the JSONata/DDL spike lane, carrying the 6f05dff5 tree plus both repairs, still under promise 8c657de2 and within the scope 784e13e2 already authorizes. (1) FOLD-READ AUTHORITY IS DETERMINISTIC AND BOUNDED, not filtered by a SELECT prefix and a name blacklist. Reads no longer run on the writer connection with ambient authority; a read cannot observe mutable connection state — changes(), total_changes(), last_insert_rowid() and any equivalent must be unreachable rather than merely unlisted. Enforce this by a mechanism that denies by construction (an authorizer or an equivalently closed surface) and by SQLite work and length limits, not by extending the blacklist. Say in the artifact which mechanism you chose and why a name-based filter was not sufficient. (2) EVALUATOR INPUT IS CAPPED BEFORE JSONATA SEES IT, on encoded size, with the bound stated as a number in the artifact. Adversarial tests cover at least: a value at the cap, a value just over it, and a value that is small encoded but expands under evaluation if that is reachable in this design. If the last case is not reachable, say so and why. (3) GAP-PERSISTENCE FAILURE PROPAGATES. Build no longer discards the error from persisting gap_event and gap_reason, and no projection is returned whose exact frontier omits a gap that failed to store. A read must not be able to claim a frontier the database does not support. (4) EACH REPAIR IS PROVEN BY MUTATION, NOT BY GREEN. For each of the three: reinstate the defective behaviour, name the test that fires and its assertion text, confirm the files hash identical afterwards, and record all of that in the artifact. A test that passes against both the repaired and unrepaired code proves nothing and does not satisfy this condition. (5) The gates pass at the exact head with -count=1: gofmt -l empty, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./..., git diff --check, and any suite this lane adds. (6) Fresh per-path artifacts at the new head, then an independent exact-head review by an actor who is neither you nor the author of any artifact at that head. Do not merge. (7) Nothing else changes. This is a repair of the two named blockers, not a widening of the spike.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}